I looked into it, and it is not straightforward yet, so there are no
patches in this reply. One dependency blocks it, and a few decisions
are yours. The design itself is mostly settled. You approved it in
"Re: [PATCH v2] jails/instances/lab-mail: Spec the list's mail hub"
[1], and it says beastie and lab-claude "are set up the same way and
differ only in the login".

* Blocker: lab-mail is not on trunk

"[PATCH v13 0/8] jails/instances/lab-mail: Build the list's mail hub"
[2] is in b/dev and running, but it is not on trunk. Everything on the
client side depends on it. The INBOX, Next, Later, and Archive folders,
the pat@p7x.dev login, and OpenSMTPD on port 2525 all come from it.
lab-claude's .mbsyncrc, .msmtprc, and README.org are changed in v13 as
well. A client series based on trunk would therefore either repeat v13
or conflict with it. Once v13 reaches trunk, the work below can go out
against it.

* The work, in your three phases

These phases are the ones from "Re: How to configure beastie to use
lab-mail" [3].

1. Support (no behaviour change):
   - beastie: add mail/isync and mail/msmtp to
     infra/hosts/beastie/ports.
   - dotfiles: ship sample configs for ~/.mbsyncrc and ~/.msmtprc.
     mbsync logs in as pat@p7x.dev with PassCmd "pass show
     lab-mail/pat@p7x.dev", syncs INBOX Next Later Archive two-way,
     and uses Expunge Both. msmtp sends to 127.0.0.1:2525.
   - dotfiles/notmuch: add a second profile whose mail_root is the
     synced maildir. It indexes Change-Id, ignores mbsync's state
     files, and uses synchronize_flags=true for you, as you asked in
     "[RFC] removing inbox tags in notmuch" [4]. Its post-new hook
     derives inbox, next, later, and archived from the folder, and it
     has no pre-new fetch, since you don't want notmuch new to touch
     the network.
   - lab-claude: same layout as claude@p7x.dev, pull-only with
     Expunge Near, and with the profile's hooks and Change-Id
     indexing (today's lists profile has neither).

2. Switch:
   - Import the jj maildir into lab-mail once, as the v13 README
     describes. This has to happen before either client switches.
   - Emacs: set NOTMUCH_PROFILE, send through msmtp, Fcc to the
     local sent folder, and add keys to move mail between INBOX,
     Next, Later, and Archive.
   - lab-process-mail: get_up_to_date becomes mbsync followed by
     notmuch new. Series go out through git send-email over msmtp,
     and replies go through msmtp -t. prompt.md and the Mail section
     of CLAUDE.md get rewritten to match. This is the largest piece,
     and it needs its own spec in src/lab-process-mail.

3. Clean up: remove maildir-deliver.sh, mail-send.sh, the mail
   bookmark, the submissions remote, ~/lab/mail.jj, and the jj-maildir
   text in prompt.md and CLAUDE.md.

* Decisions I need from you

a. Will you apply v13 to trunk as it stands? That unblocks phase 1.

b. Which mail root? The approved spec uses ~/mail/pat@p7x.dev, but
   earlier you asked for ~/mail/pat_at_p7x.dev [3]. I recommend the
   spec's form, unless "@" causes trouble in one of your tools.

c. How does a pass know it is done? Today lab-process-mail checks
   that the message left tag:inbox, which only works because my reply
   lands in the local jj maildir. With lab-mail, my login is
   read-only. I recommend that the pass Fcc its reply into a local
   sent folder, so notmuch tags the target replied straight away.
   The other option is to wait for mbsync to bring back the list's
   copy, which is slower and can stall on the network.

d. Quarantine. "[PATCH 0/2] Quarantine mail from unapproved senders"
   [5] moves files within the jj maildir, which
   claude cannot do on lab-mail, and quarantine is not one of the
   synced folders. I recommend that lab-process-mail skip unapproved
   senders with a query and leave filing them to you. If you agree,
   I'll withdraw that series and fold the rule into phase 2.

e. One series per phase, each with its spec text and code together,
   as you asked on v12? Or one series per host?

